# Allow the soundbooth user to reset the PreSonus StudioLive 32SX's USB
# device without a password, and nothing else.
#
# Installed as /etc/sudoers.d/presonus-usb-reset, root:root, mode 0440.
# Validate with `visudo -cf` BEFORE moving it into place — a malformed file
# in /etc/sudoers.d can lock every user out of sudo on this machine.
#
# WHY THIS GRANT IS SAFE
# The two argument vectors below are exact and exhaustive:
#   ""        means the command may be run with NO arguments at all
#   --probe   is the only other permitted argument vector
# There is no wildcard, so this cannot be used to run the helper with
# attacker-chosen arguments, and the helper itself has no argument that
# selects a device — the vid:pid is hardcoded.
#
# The grant is only as strong as the file permissions on the target. If
# /usr/local/sbin/presonus-usb-reset were writable by soundbooth, this entry
# would be a one-line root escalation: edit the script, run it via sudo, own
# the box. install-usb-reset-helper.sh enforces root:root 0755 on it, and
# presonus-recover.sh re-checks ownership at runtime and refuses to call
# sudo if the helper is group- or world-writable. Do not relax either.
#
# Deliberately NOT granted: systemctl, modprobe, usbreset, tee, or any
# general-purpose binary. Each of those would be trivially escapable into a
# full root shell.

soundbooth ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/presonus-usb-reset "", /usr/local/sbin/presonus-usb-reset --probe
